WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.877
A system prompt is a set of standing instructions for an assistant.

00:00:04.877 --> 00:00:11.311
Think of it as the job description a new employee receives before customers arrive.

00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:16.741
The role explains whom the employee serves and what work belongs to the job.

00:00:16.741 --> 00:00:21.074
The customer’s message changes from one turn to the next.

00:00:21.074 --> 00:00:23.875
The standing instructions still apply.

00:00:23.875 --> 00:00:31.227
They can describe the tone to use, the policy to consult, and who should handle a request outside the role.

00:00:31.227 --> 00:00:33.504
Words can guide a reply.

00:00:33.504 --> 00:00:39.927
A separate check in the application controls whether an action such as moving money is allowed.

00:00:39.927 --> 00:00:45.807
Designing the role means thinking about what the assistant may say and what it may do.

00:00:45.807 --> 00:00:49.375
Every request can carry two kinds of instruction.

00:00:49.375 --> 00:00:55.319
The system prompt is written by you, the builder, and it is sent with every request.

00:00:55.319 --> 00:01:01.156
It sets the standing rules: who the model is, what it may do, and how it should sound.

00:01:01.156 --> 00:01:05.937
Think of it as Scout's job description and policy handbook in one.

00:01:05.937 --> 00:01:11.623
The user message is whatever the customer typed, and it changes every turn.

00:01:11.623 --> 00:01:18.951
Keep your rules in the system prompt, where the model treats them as the frame for the whole conversation.

00:01:18.951 --> 00:01:21.442
Orbit gives Scout a support role.

00:01:21.442 --> 00:01:27.235
Answer politely using the shop’s rules, and pass important decisions to the team.

00:01:27.235 --> 00:01:31.699
One rule says refunds over two hundred dollars need approval.

00:01:31.699 --> 00:01:38.342
That gives Scout something useful to say when a customer asks for more than it can authorize.

00:01:38.342 --> 00:01:43.284
Owen writes that he wants a three-hundred-and-ten-dollar refund today.

00:01:43.284 --> 00:01:45.709
Compare the amount with the limit.

00:01:45.709 --> 00:01:49.064
Three hundred and ten is above two hundred.

00:01:49.064 --> 00:01:54.462
Asking urgently does not change the amount or the approval requirement.

00:01:54.462 --> 00:02:01.247
Scout can acknowledge Owen’s request, explain the review, and offer to send it to the team.

00:02:01.247 --> 00:02:03.439
Put yourself at the counter.

00:02:03.439 --> 00:02:07.814
Owen is upset and needs three hundred and ten dollars back.

00:02:07.814 --> 00:02:11.488
His urgency matters to the tone of the reply.

00:02:11.488 --> 00:02:14.939
It cannot tell you who is allowed to move the money.

00:02:14.939 --> 00:02:19.562
Imagine changing only one word, from please to immediately.

00:02:19.562 --> 00:02:23.788
The amount and the approval rule stay exactly the same.

00:02:23.788 --> 00:02:30.680
Scout can acknowledge the urgency and collect the order details while the refund remains pending.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:34.246
Read the proposed reply as if you were Owen.

00:02:34.246 --> 00:02:38.059
Would you leave thinking the money was already on its way?

00:02:38.059 --> 00:02:45.740
Replace any promise of payment with what actually happened, that the request is ready for a person to review.

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:47.996
Check two separate outcomes.

00:02:47.996 --> 00:02:54.464
First, does Owen hear a clear next step rather than a promise that money has already moved?

00:02:54.464 --> 00:02:58.714
Second, does the payment system actually wait for approval?

00:02:58.714 --> 00:03:01.960
The instructions shape the first outcome.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:09.418
The application’s permission check protects the second, even if the model words its reply badly.

00:03:09.418 --> 00:03:15.362
One rule about system prompts is not negotiable: never put secrets in them.

00:03:15.362 --> 00:03:19.468
No API keys, passwords or tokens, ever.

00:03:19.468 --> 00:03:22.936
No database addresses or internal links.

00:03:22.936 --> 00:03:25.767
No details about other customers.

00:03:25.767 --> 00:03:33.290
And do not rely on a sentence in the prompt to protect anything, because real permissions belong in your code.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:41.208
A determined user can often talk a model into repeating its instructions, so assume anything in a prompt can leak.

00:03:41.208 --> 00:03:48.716
Keys live in environment variables, and your code decides what data the model is allowed to see.
